sentences.text:
The brief facts of the present case are that on 20.03.2002, the Food Inspector Ms. Usha Kiran and Sh. Baljeet Singh, Field Assistant under the supervision of Local Health Authority/SDM Sh.M.A.
Abid, reached at the premises of appellant i.e. M/s Shree General Store at Tanki Road, Bhajanpura,
Delhi where accused Bhagwan Das, Vendor cum Proprietor was found conducting business of various food articles including Dal Malka Masoor which were lying stored/exposed for sale for human consumption ;
CA no. 8700/16 Bhagwan Das Vs. Food Inspector (PFA) Pages 2 of 10 - that FI disclosed her identity and expressed her intention to purchase a sample of commodity from the vendor which was lying in an open plastic bag bearing no label declaration, to which he agreed ;
- that thereafter as per the prescribed procedure under the PFA Act and Rules after giving notice to the vendor/appellant and offer towards the sale price of sample to which he agreed and accepted the sale consideration, sample was then lifted ;
- that each sample was separately packed, fastened, marked, sealed and necessary documents including Notice as per Form-VI in the presence of appellant/accused alongwith Panchnama etc was prepared at the spot ; the price of the sample commodity was paid to the vendor ;
- that thereafter one counterpart of sample was sent to Public Analyst (PA) in intact condition and other two counterparts were deposited with SDM/LHA ;
- that vide report dated 08.04.2002, the PA found the sample to be adulterated on the ground that it was coloured with synthetic colouring matter "Sunset Yellow FCF" ;
- that upon receipt of report, SDM/LHA directed investigation which was carried out by Food Inspector and after completion of investigation sanction under Section 20 of the PFA Act was obtained from the Director PFA.

