The complaint was then filed in the court on 31.10.2003 alleging violation of Section 2(ia)(a),(j) and (m) of PFA Act r/w Rules 23,28 & 29 of PFA Rules, as punishable under Section 7/16(1A) of PFA. Act.
That the complaint was filed in writing by a public servant, hence, recording of pre-summoning evidence was dispensed with and the accused was summoned by the learned trial court vide order dated 31.10.2003 for 01.06.2004 to face trial.
- After filing appearance appellant/accused adopted the procedure available as per the statute ;
pre-charge evidence was recorded ; and on the basis of testimony of prosecution witnesses, charge was framed against the accused to which he pleaded not guilty ; thereafter statement of accused was recorded and after seeking opportunity to lead DE, no witness was examined or produced ; final arguments were heard and accused was held guilty by the learned trial court observing that it stands established by prosecution beyond reasonable doubt that the accused had sold adulterated food in violation of Section 2(ia)(a),(j) and (m) of PFA Act read with Rules 23,28 and 29 of the PFA Rules and has committed the offence punishable under Section 7/16(1A) of PFA Act and was thus convicted for the said offences by the learned trial court.
- In the order on quantum of sentence, appellant was sentenced to undergo simple imprisonment for one year and to pay a fine of Rs. 30,000/- and in default of payment of fine to undergo simple imprisonment for 15 days for offence punishable u/s 16(1A) r/w Section 7 of PFA Act, 1954 .
